<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trondheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trondheim is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the historic centre of the Trøndelag region. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> of 1658, Denmark-Norway ceded the province of Trondheim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> and other territories. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> note these gains as marking the height of Swedish territorial expansion, though Trondheim was returned to Denmark-Norway in 1660.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trondheim.jpg" alt="Trondheim" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trondheim Havn from Trondheim, Norway · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trondheim_overview_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Trondheim lies on the south shore of Trondheim Fjord, at the mouth of the river Nidelva, with an excellent and sheltered harbour. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the river was deep enough for most boats, but in the mid-17th century an avalanche of mud and stones made it less navigable and partly ruined the harbour. Despite its northerly latitude it has a mild climate. Around the summer solstice the nights are light enough that no outdoor lighting is needed between 23 May and 19 July, while at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> the sun stands only slightly more than 3 degrees above the horizon at midday.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement was founded in 997 as a trading post by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> king Olav Tryggvason, who named it Kaupangen, meaning market place. Shortly afterwards it came to be called Nidaros, the outlet of the river Nid. The district around it, Trondheim, meant the home of the Trønder people, and Trøndelag the law area of the Trønder. The city served as the king's seat and was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> until 1217. From 1152 to 1537 it was the seat of the Catholic Archdiocese of Nidaros; it then became, and has remained, the seat of the Lutheran Diocese of Nidaros.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a>, begun in 1070, is the most important Gothic monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>'s most important Christian pilgrimage site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, with routes leading to it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> and from the Jämtland and Värmland regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Sverre Sigurdsson built the fortification Sverresborg in 1182 and 1183; it was burned in 1188 and had been restored by 1197. Kristiansten Fortress, built from 1681 to 1684, repelled invading Swedes in 1718 and was decommissioned in 1816. During the German occupation of Norway in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city held a submarine base for the 13th U-boat Flotilla, and Vollan District Jail was used for prisoners of war and political prisoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Trondheim is a city and municipality in Trøndelag county, the third most populous municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. It was incorporated as a city on 1 January 1838, and its present municipality was formed in 1964 by merger with four neighbouring municipalities, and enlarged again in 2020. The Norwegian University of Science and Technology and the research foundation SINTEF have their headquarters there, and the cathedral stands beside the Archbishop's Palace in the city centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trondheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian III’s accession charter of 1536 declared that Norway should no longer be a separate kingdom but a part of the Danish realm, a change made effective when the last Catholic archbishop of <b>Nidaros</b>, Olav Engelbrektsson, fled the country in 1537.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Church, organised under the archbishopric of <b>Nidaros</b> (Trondheim) from 1152–1153, built stone cathedrals and stave churches such as Urnes and Borgund and became the largest landowner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…(1658), imposed by Charles X Gustav after his celebrated March across the Belts over the frozen straits, Denmark–Norway ceded Scania, Blekinge, Halland, Bornholm, Bohuslän and Trøndelag, the region of <b>Trondheim</b>; Bornholm and Trøndelag were returned in 1660, but the remaining provinces fixed the southern borders of modern Sweden.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The earls took their name from Lade, a farm near modern <b>Trondheim</b> in Trøndelag.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-lade">Earldom of Lade</a></li></ul></section>
